diff --git a/docs/runtime/presentation-provider.md b/docs/runtime/presentation-provider.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9b0a629
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/runtime/presentation-provider.md
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# External framebuffer presentation provider
+
+The Windows framebuffer window normally uses GDI or its opt-in D3D11
+presenter. A downstream host can instead register one
+`RecompPresentProvider` with
+`xbox_FramebufferWindowSetPresentProvider` before the framebuffer window
+starts. No provider is registered by default, so existing presentation is
+unchanged.
+
+The provider structure is copied at registration. Its `context` remains
+borrowed through shutdown. `initialize`, `present`, and `shutdown` execute on
+the framebuffer-window thread. `output_size` may execute concurrently on a
+renderer thread; shutdown waits for an in-flight output query before invoking
+the provider's shutdown callback. Registration, replacement, or removal is
+rejected after initialization begins.
+
+`present` receives the stable CPU snapshot, its dimensions, the publication
+frame ID, and an `allow_source` flag. A provider may select a separately
+published GPU source only when `allow_source` is nonzero and its frame ID
+matches. A zero flag requires the CPU pixels, including for pinned
+framebuffers, PVIDEO composition, and presenter-source capture. The callback
+returns 1 when it paced the framebuffer loop, 0 when it did not, and -1 after
+reporting a fatal failure. Initialization or presentation failure stops
+instead of silently selecting GDI or D3D11.
+
+The provider owns its API-specific window surface, swap chain, resize,
+acquisition, composition, final presentation, and device-loss handling. It
+does not replace guest framebuffer publication, frame identity, CPU/PVIDEO
+fallback, capture, or backend readback.
